  "_exec_summary": [
    {
      "ESSeqNum": 0,
      "FacilityID": 1000120417,
      "SummaryText": "American Spraytech owns the facility located at 25 ImClone in Branchburg, NJ. The previous owner, Falcon Safety Products, stopped their operations in September 2023. Since this is a predictive filing, the dates of audits, PHA updates, and other required compliance dates have not been changed since last submittal, but all occurred within 5 years of the stopping of Falcon\u0092s operations. The intent of this resubmission is to prepare for restarting the operation of the facility. This is a predictive filing notice for the intended startup date of 07/01/2025. There are several dates in this filing that will be out of compliance, these activities will be completed and updated before the startup of the facility. Another filing will be initiated at that point in order to update all the compliance dates.\n\nTherefore, American Spraytech is re-submitting the RMP with hazardous substance quantities estimated for when operations begin. The basic operation of the facility, including quantity and types of chemicals used (difluoroethane), storage systems, and safety equipment is currently predicted to be similar to Falcon Safety Products\u0092 operation.\n\nOnce exact chemical quantities and systems are finalized, American Spraytech will re-submit this RMP with updated information, including PHAs, emergency response protocols, and all other items listed below.\n\n1.\tAccidental Release Prevention Policies:\nIn addition to 25 ImClone, American Spraytech also owns and operates facilities located at 181 and 205 Meister Avenue in Branchburg, NJ which are currently subject to the RMP requirements of Part 68. The facilities handle Liquefied Petroleum Gases (propane, isobutane, normal butane, difluoroethane and methyl ether) that are considered hazardous by the EPA. These are flammable gases, so it is necessary to follow safety precautions during handling to prevent human exposure and reduce health threats to our workers and the nearby community.\n\nSafety depends on:\n(1) The manner in which we handle th"
    },
    {
      "ESSeqNum": 1,
      "FacilityID": 1000120417,
      "SummaryText": "ese products;\n(2) The safety devices inherent in the design of the facility;\n(3) Operating and maintenance procedures; and\n(4) Employee training. The President has overall authority for the safety program at the Branchburg facility.\n\n2. The Stationary Source and Regulated Substances handled:  The primary purpose of this facility is to receive and store Liquefied Petroleum Gases (difluoroethane) and then use these materials as propellants in aerosol containers. These Liquefied Petroleum Gases are the only materials that will be handled at the facility that are subject to the EPA's Risk Management Program Regulation.\n\n3. The General Accidental Release Prevention Program and the Specific Prevention Steps: The facility had developed a program level 3 accident release prevention program for the hydrocarbon systems that complies with OSHA's Process Safety Management Standard and EPA's Risk Management Program Regulation. \nThe prevention program consists of the following elements:\n(1) Employee Participation Program\n(2) Process Safety Information\n(3) Process Hazard Analysis\n(4) Operating Procedures\n(5) Training Program\n(6) Contractor Safety Program\n(7) Pre-Startup Safety Review Procedures\n(8) Mechanical Integrity Program\n(9) Hot Work Permit Procedures\n(10) Management of Change Procedures\n(11) Incident Investigation Procedures\n(12) Compliance Audit Procedures\n(13) Trade Secrets Procedures\n(14) Management System\n\n4. Five-Year Accident History There have not been any accidents in the previous five years at the Branchburg facility that resulted in significant onsite or offsite consequences.\n\n5. The Emergency Action Program: The emergency action plan for this facility consists of evacuating personnel to a designated assembly area. Our emergency action plan details employee actions and includes procedures for notification of the local Fire Department and the local LEPC. If the release is beyond the capabilities of facility personnel, representatives from the Fire Department would"
    },
    {
      "ESSeqNum": 2,
      "FacilityID": 1000120417,
      "SummaryText": "be called to respond to the incident. The emergency action plan, once finalized prior to beginning of operations, will be coordinated with the Fire Department.\n\n6. Planned Changes to Improve Safety: Process Hazard Analyses, operating procedures, facility-specific training, and all other elements of American Spraytech\u0092s comprehensive accidental release prevention program, will be evaluated, updated, and re-submitted prior to beginning of operations on 07/01/2025."
    }
  ]
